# Defaults for `act` (https://github.com/nektos/act), which runs the workflows
# in .github/workflows/ locally in containers. See docs/developer.md.
#
# act's own default image for ubuntu-latest is `node:16-buster-slim`, which has
# no sudo, no apt worth the name and no git. Every job here needs at least a
# real distribution, and release.yml's full-suite job runs
# `sudo apt-get install gcc-multilib`, so the medium `catthehacker` image is
# the smallest one that works. It is about 1GB and is pulled once.
-P ubuntu-latest=catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest

# Reuse the container between runs rather than building it from scratch each
# time. This is what makes a second run of a job take seconds instead of
# minutes: the Go module cache, the apt lists and the toolchain setup-go
# downloads all survive. `make ci-clean` throws them away when that goes stale.
--reuse

# Do not set GITHUB_TOKEN to "" here to quieten the warnings. act uses that
# token to `git clone` the actions themselves, and an empty one is worse than
# an absent one: it turns anonymous cloning into a failed password
# authentication, and release.yml's full-suite job dies fetching
# docker/build-push-action. If GitHub starts rate-limiting the clones, pass a
# real one for the run instead:
#
#     act -W .github/workflows/release.yml -j full-suite -s GITHUB_TOKEN="$(gh auth token)"
